1.2. Quotients of aﬃne varieties by reductive group actions.
Deﬁnition 1.18. Given an algebraic group G and a G-variety X, a geometric quotient of X by
G consists of a morphism π : X →Y satisfying the following properties:
(i) π is surjective, and its ﬁbers are exactly the G-orbits in X.
(ii) A subset U ⊂Y is open if and only if π−1(U) is open.
(iii) For any open subset U ⊂Y , the comorphism π# yields an isomorphism C[U] ∼= C[π−1(U)]H.
Under these assumptions, the topological space Y may be identiﬁed with the orbit space X/G
equipped with the quotient topology, in view of (i) and (ii). Moreover, the structure of variety on
Y is uniquely deﬁned by (iii) (which may be rephrased as the equality of sheaves OY = (π∗OX)G).
In particular, if X is irreducible, then so is Y , and we have the equality of function ﬁelds C(Y ) =
C(X)G.
For example, the geometric quotient of a linear algebraic group by a closed subgroup (acting
via right multiplication) exists by Theorem 1.16. In general, a geometric quotient need not exist,
