+
+[Burgess, §2.1, equations (25)–(26)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0701053)
+
+[Neubert, §2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-ph/0512222)
+
+[Criado–Pérez-Victoria, §§3 and 5.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1811.09413)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** the field, couplings, dimensions, cutoff and degree bounds, formal parameter, action, and boundary convention in the Example.
+
+[A1] [[def-axiom-of-choice]]: full AC is adopted in the current A-page prerequisite chain.
+
+[F1] [[def-aqft-local-operator-and-dimension]]: fixed derivative, polynomial-degree and engineering-weight bounds define a finite jet-polynomial operator space, with derivative weight one and field weight given by its declared engineering dimension; the example adjoins the explicitly declared finite-degree mass spurion.
+
+[F2] [[def-aqft-operator-basis-quotient]]: an invariant operator quotient is the invariant vector space modulo its specified relation subspace, and it records precisely that declared relation space.
+
+[F3] [[prop-aqft-integration-by-parts-eom-and-field-redefinition-redundancy]]: under a compact-support no-boundary convention and the displayed variational-derivative convention, the identity-leading map $\Psi_k(\phi)=\phi-\epsilon^kF[\phi]$ is formally invertible through the retained order and changes $S_\epsilon$ by $-\epsilon^k\mathcal I(F^a\mathcal E_a^{(0)})+O(\epsilon^{k+1})$.
+
+## Verification
+
+1.1 For a compactly supported variation $h$, the kinetic variation is $-\mathcal I(\partial_\mu h\,\partial^\mu\phi)=\mathcal I(h\Box\phi)$, since $\partial_\mu(h\partial^\mu\phi)$ integrates to zero. The potential variation is $\mathcal I(h(-m^2\phi-g\phi^3/6))$. Thus $DS_0[\phi](h)=\mathcal I(h\mathcal E_0)$ with the displayed $\mathcal E_0$. [given, algebra]
+
+1.2 The field has weight one and each derivative has weight one, so $\mathcal O_6$ has weight six; $m^2\phi^4$ and $g\phi^6$ also have weight six. Their derivative orders and field degrees lie within the stated cutoffs, so the displayed identity concerns elements of the declared finite operator space. [given, F1, algebra]
+
+2.1 The product rule gives $\partial_\mu(\phi^3\partial^\mu\phi)=3\phi^2\partial_\mu\phi\partial^\mu\phi+\phi^3\Box\phi$. The boundary convention therefore yields $\mathcal I(\phi^3\Box\phi)=-3\mathcal I(\mathcal O_6)$. [given, step 1.2, algebra]
+
+2.2 Before specialization, substituting $\widehat{\mathcal E}_0=\Box\phi-M_s^2\phi-g\phi^3/6$ into step 2.1 gives $\mathcal I(\phi^3\widehat{\mathcal E}_0)=-3\mathcal I(\mathcal O_6)-\mathcal I(M_s^2\phi^4)-(g/6)\mathcal I(\phi^6)$. Each density has weight six, and the relation subspace contains this leading-equation density and the divergence from step 2.1. The quotient definition [F2] therefore gives the displayed class identity. [given, F2, step 2.1, algebra]
+
+3.1 Put $a=C/(3\Lambda^2)$ and use [F3] with $k=1$ and $F[\phi]=-a\phi^3$, so its map $\phi-\epsilon F[\phi]$ is $\phi+\epsilon a\phi^3$. Its units are those of $\phi$, and its first-order action change is $-\epsilon\mathcal I(F\mathcal E_0)=\epsilon a\mathcal I(\phi^3\mathcal E_0)$, as also follows from step 1.1. Since $\epsilon^2=0$, substituting the shifted field into the order-$\epsilon$ term changes that term only by zero in the truncated ring. [given, A1, F3, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+3.2 Specialize $M_s=m$ in step 2.2, insert that identity into step 3.1, and add the original $+\epsilon(C/\Lambda^2)\mathcal I(\mathcal O_6)$ term. Its coefficient cancels because $3a=C/\Lambda^2$, leaving exactly the quartic and sextic shifts displayed in the Example. [given, step 2.2, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
