        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Use the complex Hilbert-space convention that the inner product is linear in its first variable and conjugate-linear in its second. Let $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $\\sigma^0=I_2$, $\\sigma^1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma^2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and $\\sigma^3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$. Define $\\operatorname{Herm}_2(\\mathbb C)=\\{H\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):H^\\dagger=H\\}$ and $\\Phi:\\mathbb R^{1,3}\\to\\operatorname{Herm}_2(\\mathbb C)$ by $\\Phi(x)=-\\sigma^\\mu x_\\mu$. For $g\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)=\\{g\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):\\det g=1\\}$, define $\\Lambda_g$ by $\\Phi(\\Lambda_gx)=g\\Phi(x)g^\\dagger$; the real-linear map $\\Phi$ is an isomorphism because $I_2,\\sigma^1,\\sigma^2,\\sigma^3$ form a basis of $\\operatorname{Herm}_2(\\mathbb C)$, and $\\det\\Phi(x)=-\\eta_{\\mu\\nu}x^\\mu x^\\nu$ is preserved. Congruence by $g$ preserves positive-semidefinite Hermitian matrices, so $\\Lambda_g$ preserves the chosen future cone. Let $\\Lambda_g^\\vee$ be the dual action on covectors, $(\\Lambda_g^\\vee p)(x)=p(\\Lambda_g^{-1}x)$. The spin-cover Poincare group is $\\mathcal P=\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)\\ltimes_\\Lambda\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with product $(g,a)(h,b)=(gh,a+\\Lambda_gb)$.\n\nA **positive unitary super-Poincare representation** in this item is the following data. It includes a nonzero complex Hilbert space $\\mathcal H$, a bounded self-adjoint grading $\\Gamma$ with $\\Gamma^2=I$, and a strongly continuous unitary representation $U$ of $\\mathcal P$ commuting with $\\Gamma$. It also includes a regular projection-valued measure $E$ on the Borel subsets of the covector space $(\\mathbb R^{1,3})^*$ such that\n\n$$U(1,a)=\\Phi_E(p\\mapsto e^{i p(a)}),\\qquad U(g,0)E(B)U(g,0)^*=E(\\Lambda_g^\\vee B),\\qquad E(\\mathcal C_+^*)=I,$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi_E$ is the bounded Borel PVM integral, $\\mathcal C_+^*=\\{p:p^0=-p_0\\ge |\\vec p|\\}$ is the closed future cone in covector coordinates, and $p(a)=p_\\mu a^\\mu$. The PVM functional calculus makes every translation unitary commute with every $E(B)$. For each coordinate define the self-adjoint translation generator\n\n$$P_\\mu=p_\\mu(E),\\qquad D(P_\\mu)=\\left\\{\\psi\\in\\mathcal H:\\int |p_\\mu|^2\\,dE_\\psi(p)<\\infty\\right\\},\\qquad E_\\psi(B)=\\langle E(B)\\psi,\\psi\\rangle.$$\n\nHere $p_\\mu(E)$ is the unbounded PVM integral with its truncation domain; the real-valued integral is self-adjoint under Countable Choice. Thus $P^0=\\eta^{0\\nu}P_\\nu$ is nonnegative. The joint PVM is part of the representation data; no joint spectral measure is inferred from formal commutators on a dense domain.\n\nFor each $I,\\alpha$, the supercharge $Q^I_\\alpha$ is a closed densely defined operator on $\\mathcal H$ and $\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha I}=(Q^I_\\alpha)^*$ is its actual Hilbert adjoint, with exactly the adjoint domain. The central generators $Z^{IJ}$ and $\\bar Z_{IJ}$ are represented by closed densely defined operators with $\\bar Z_{IJ}=(Z^{IJ})^*$ as an actual Hilbert adjoint. On a common dense graded linear domain $\\mathcal D$, contained in the domains of every generator product in the bracket table, the operator supercommutator is the displayed algebra bracket. In particular,\n\n$$\\{Q^I_\\alpha,\\bar Q_{\\dot\\beta J}\\}=-2\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\beta}P_\\mu\\delta^I{}_J,\\qquad \\{Q^I_\\alpha,Q^J_\\beta\\}=2\\epsilon_{\\alpha\\beta}Z^{IJ},\\qquad \\{\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha I},\\bar Q_{\\dot\\beta J}\\}=2\\epsilon_{\\dot\\alpha\\dot\\beta}\\bar Z_{IJ}.$$\n\nThe domain $\\mathcal D$ is invariant under those generators and under $U(\\mathcal P)$. The even group acts on the charge domains exactly: for every $g\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)$, conjugation by $U(g,0)$ sends each closed charge to the corresponding fundamental or dotted spinor transform as an equality of operators, including equality of domains. The central generators transform as Lorentz scalars, commute with translations, and commute on $\\mathcal D$ with all supercharges; these relations also include their domain invariance.\n\nFor every Borel set $B\\subseteq(\\mathbb R^{1,3})^*$ and every charge $Q$ or $Q^*$, require the reducing-projection/domain conditions\n\n$$E(B)D(Q)\\subseteq D(Q),\\qquad Q E(B)\\psi=E(B)Q\\psi\\quad(\\psi\\in D(Q)),$$\n\nand the same conditions with $Q^*$ in place of $Q$. Require also $\\Gamma E(B)=E(B)\\Gamma$ for every Borel $B$. In particular this supplies integrated translation covariance with domain invariance; a relation $[P_\\mu,Q]=0$ on a common core alone is not substituted for it. A closed graded subspace $K$ is invariant when $U(g,a)K=K$ for all $(g,a)\\in\\mathcal P$ and each charge and adjoint maps $D(Q)\\cap K$ into $K$, with those intersections dense in $K$. The representation is irreducible when its only such invariant subspaces are $\\{0\\}$ and $\\mathcal H$.",
